[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Motion: CSS3 Transforms, Transitions, and Keyframe Animations"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic user interfaces rely heavily on motion design to guide user attention, provide feedback, and enhance overall interactivity. CSS3 introduced native capabilities for manipulating elements without relying on JavaScript. This tutorial covers the three pillars of CSS motion: Transforms, Transitions, and Keyframe Animations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. CSS3 Transforms"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transforms allow you to alter the coordinate space of the CSS visual formatting model. Using the transform property, you can translate, rotate, scale, and skew elements in 2D or 3D space."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"translate(x, y): Moves an element along the X and Y axes."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"scale(x, y): Resizes an element horizontally and vertically."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"rotate(angle): Rotates an element around a fixed point."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"skew(ax, ay): Distorts an element along the X and Y axes."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":".box {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background-color: #3498db;
  transform: rotate(45deg) scale(1.2);
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. CSS3 Transitions"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transitions provide a way to control animation speed when changing CSS properties. Instead of having property changes take effect immediately, you can allow the changes to occur over a specified duration."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"transition-property: Specifies the CSS property to animate."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"transition-duration: Defines how long the transition takes."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"transition-timing-function: Sets the acceleration curve (e.g., ease, linear, cubic-bezier)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"transition-delay: Specifies a wait time before the transition starts."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":".button {
  background-color: #2ecc71;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease-in-out, transform 0.2s linear;
}

.button:hover {
  background-color: #27ae60;
  transform: translateY(-5px);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"3. CSS3 Keyframe Animations"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"While transitions are limited to state changes (like hovering), keyframe animations give you precise control over intermediate steps in a sequence. You define the animation steps using the @keyframes rule and then apply it to an element."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"@keyframes pulse {
  0% {
    transform: scale(1);
    opacity: 1;
  }
  50% {
    transform: scale(1.1);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  100% {
    transform: scale(1);
    opacity: 1;
  }
}

.pulsing-element {
  animation: pulse 2s infinite ease-in-out;
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Performance"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always animate properties that trigger GPU acceleration, specifically transform and opacity."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid animating layout-triggering properties like width, height, margin, or top/left, as they cause expensive layout reflows."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining transforms, smooth transitions, and robust keyframe animations, you can create performant, highly engaging modern web interfaces."}]
